{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S151-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"2457b85bfc6e2bc08dca2084ef4b51c8ca847e331f8ed02bf03eee6237c6269f\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 calls backward walking a family Sunday practice; A3 incorporates denial into that tradition.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 directly absorbs B2's denial; A5 addresses B4's tea, but 'before remembering' obscures the sequence.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants to avoid spilling tea, though B2 offers only a generic denial of shared history.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds a tea-finishing custom but barely increases the commitment established in A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies family dessert and backward walking; B4 adds a playable cup of tea.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'before the backward part' neatly captures denial; A5's remembering phrase is less clean.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 80\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Walk backwards to dessert, like every family Sunday.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've never done that.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You always say that before the backward part.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll spill my tea. Can you carry it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Of course. That's why we link elbows. Gran always said nobody should reach pudding alone.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats an odd family activity as inherited shared practice and turns practical accommodation into another established custom.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic insistence on an absurd rule would demand obedience without claiming a shared past.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 invokes family Sundays, A3 remembers B's supposed habitual denial, and revised A5 attributes linked elbows to Gran.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative recruits a relative through bodily cooperation; alternative b preserves an absent ancestor's place through hospitality.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original B4–A5 connection was understandable but muddled. The revision keeps the setting and makes B4's request for help produce a further tradition in A5. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 invent shared history), logic 5 (A5 accepts B4's carrying request), relationship 4 (B4 negotiates assistance), heightening 4 (A5 adds linked participation), specificity 5 (A1 dessert and B4 tea), brevity/wit 4 (A5's family maxim earns the extension); total 92. Hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 introduces Gran as invented family history; its warmth requires sincere delivery rather than a knowing wink.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S151-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8fdf8cb7173dccd4d8d8650e868803e2307f28142e710cf0a087e66acd13dfae\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1's 'again' suggests custom, but A3 and A5 mainly elaborate the invisible ancestor's existence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's denial and A5 answers B4's window question, without changing what anyone must do.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 denies the ancestor and B4 asks why; neither supplies a personal need or practical stake.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds curtain lore rather than developing A1's immediate continuation of a tradition.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 clearly locates a chair by a window, and A5 maintains those physical details.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Not one we've seen' is economical, but its joke favors invisibility over inherited custom.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 62\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've set the invisible ancestor's chair by the window again. Pass his plate.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That's our last chair. Where does my boyfriend sit?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"On the piano stool, like every newcomer. Your father managed.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"He brought the pudding. Can't the ancestor share?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Of course. Pudding bearers sit on his lap. Put a cushion down, as usual.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A folds an impossible dinner arrangement and each accommodation into a supposedly longstanding family tradition.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Believing in an invisible person alone does not establish an old shared custom that must continue immediately.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 supplies only 'again'; revised A3 cites the father's precedent and A5 demands the customary cushion.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike alternative a's cooperative backward journey, this scene uses a host's family precedents to absorb a guest into an eccentric seating arrangement.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced exposition with a dinner seating problem; the new boyfriend and pudding are illustrative additions. B2 causes the newcomer precedent, and B4's sharing proposal causes the lap custom. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 claim precedents), logic 5 (A3 answers chair scarcity; A5 uses B4's pudding), relationship 4 (B protects a guest), heightening 4 (A5 makes sharing stranger), specificity 5 (A1/A3 establish dinner seating), brevity/wit 4 (A5 turns accommodation into tradition); total 92. Hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The invisible lap needs imaginative acceptance, but no physical action is prescribed outside the spoken scene.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S152-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b07850dd49dd1304444e220972a52ec4d6dc5b52a28733d2d45a37f525106e6d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 mentions lunch money, but A3 justifies the demand through 'You said anything' rather than the past favor's fair value.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 responds to B2's limited offer; A5's 'start earlier' sidesteps B4's inability to travel through time.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers lunch and B4 states a limit, providing a credible debtor's resistance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 supplies a time pun without increasing the repayment obligation or its practical consequences.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 names lunch money but gives no reason A wants those particular ten minutes of yesterday.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 is concise, while A5's detachable 'start earlier' joke weakens the exchange's specific logic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 51\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I covered your lunch yesterday. Bring back the ten minutes I spent queuing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll buy your lunch today.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That covers the sandwich. I'm only asking for my time back.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can queue for you today.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Thanks. Use that time to retrieve yesterday's minutes. Then we're square.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A values a concrete past favor as sufficient payment for an impossible return and calmly treats that accounting as fair.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Enforcing an impossible promise would make B's wording the source of obligation instead of A's earlier kindness.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 relies on 'anything'; revised A3 divides the sandwich from the time debt and A5 calls impossible repayment square.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a accepts a helpful offer but allocates it toward impossible repayment; alternative b argues that moving the moon is easier than moving a sofa.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Removed the promise-based justification while preserving lunch and yesterday's time. B2 produces itemized accounting; B4's queueing offer becomes work time for the miracle. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1/A3 ground fairness in the favor), logic 5 (A3 uses lunch; A5 uses queueing), relationship 4 (B offers two remedies), heightening 4 (A5 assigns the remedy to impossible collection), specificity 5 (A1 identifies the lost minutes), brevity/wit 4 (A5's 'square' completes the accounting); total 92. Hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The unchanged transfer still privileges an 'anything' promise, so its provenance cannot be safely rebound to this corrected mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S152-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b02d428aa978eed2d6a2ebe9a878780c6d545ce305afe31ca2fb5f93899fea17\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 exchanges sofa-moving for moon-moving; A3 sincerely argues that stairs make the past favor harder.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's equivalence objection; A5 uses B4's procedural question to demand reciprocal unquestioning help.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 disputes the debt's scale and B4 seeks a method, allowing 