\"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's devotion comparison is economical; A5's abstract 'measurable commitment' explains the joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You only bow to our invisible queen? I'm giving her my chair.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She already has an invisible throne.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then mine can support her feet. You hadn't considered her comfort.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll give her my cushion, then. You can sit down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Keep your cushion. She can have my bed. I'll sleep standing in case she calls.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A competes over belief in the invisible queen by making increasingly costly, unnecessary provisions for her.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Demanding devotion from B would police someone else's belief without requiring A to demonstrate greater commitment.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 assesses B's knees; revised A1 offers A's chair and A5 escalates to A's bed and standing vigil.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns B's considerate cushion offer into competitive self-sacrifice; alternative b turns a friend's practical objection into a more excessive written pledge.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Reworked the queen scene around A's own escalating demonstrations. B2 converts the chair into a footrest; B4's competing cushion offer triggers bed and vigil. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1/A5 compete through provisions), logic 5 (A3 uses throne; A5 exceeds cushion), relationship 4 (B offers comfort to both), heightening 5 (A5 sacrifices sleep), specificity 4 (A1 establishes courtly seating), brevity/wit 4 (A5 follows competitive hospitality); total 93. Hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 offers future demonstrations rather than requiring enacted movement; the spoken commitments must feel immediate and sincere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S154-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5f775b3afc0ac6cb160bf585fae2b19f0477a876f7bf5608ba30a3c13cfb3d9e\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 makes faith competitive, but A1's moon pledge never states the strange belief and A5 merely defends illegibility.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's one-signature limit; A5 reframes B4's pen-handling objection as superior certainty.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants a sufficient signature and B4 identifies a practical obstacle, but their stake remains thin.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 justifies the same two-pen action from A1 instead of adding a more unnecessary demonstration.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a pledge and A4 does not exist; B4 supplies pens, but the pledge's actual premise stays unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'one-handed faith' is compact; A5's 'beyond ordinary certainty' substitutes abstraction for escalation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You signed the pledge that the moon hears us? I'll sign with both hands.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"One signature's enough. I'm taking it outside tonight.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll use two pens. She should know which of us means it twice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That's illegible. She won't know your name.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll write it across my bedsheet. You can carry your little page.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats belief that the moon hears people as a contest and repeatedly enlarges the visible proof of personal commitment.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Saving face after poor handwriting would explain a defensive claim, but not a further unnecessary demonstration of stronger belief.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 explicitly compares whose belief counts twice, and B4's illegibility objection causes A5's bedsheet pledge.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative competes through scale and public display; alternative a competes through personal discomfort while B tries to help.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Specified the moon belief and added a planned outdoor delivery. B2's delivery plan informs A3's recipient-focused comparison; B4's legibility concern causes A5's larger medium. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 explicitly outdo B), logic 5 (A3 uses delivery; A5 fixes readability excessively), relationship 4 (B manages delivery), heightening 4 (A5 moves from pens to bedsheet), specificity 5 (A1 names the belief), brevity/wit 4 (A5's little page sustains rivalry); total 92. Hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The moon's hearing and reading are treated as compatible within the premise, without additional supernatural explanation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S155-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"703e43f2c8c1797d724703e4faa4dcf0f69eeb6cd5d6b436f34c8d55a803bd5c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A5 label successes coincidence, and A5 seeks another test, but A3 never actually compliments the cupboard.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 acknowledges B2's test without performing it in speech, leaving B4's opening result dependent on an unstated action.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 supplies a method and B4 reports success, but B has little purpose beyond demonstrating 